Looking at the net, the six triangles in the middle row form the band of the octahedron (viewed as a triangular antiprism), with the top and bottom flaps closing off the two opposite faces.

**Tracking vertices when folded:**
- Closing the band identifies the left edge (label 4) with the right edge (label 1).
- The top flap (with edge 3) folds onto the top face, so its apex meets the top vertex of the band, making edge 3 coincide with edge 2.
- The bottom flap (with edge x) folds onto the bottom face: its apex meets the bottom middle vertex of the band, so edge x (from the right bottom vertex to the flap apex) wraps onto the bottom edge of the second triangle in the row — exactly the edge labelled 5.

**Checking via vertex classes:** Label the net vertices A₀…A₃ (top row), B₀…B₃ (bottom row), P (top apex), Q (bottom apex). Folding gives identifications A₀∼A₃, B₀∼B₃, P∼A₂, Q∼B₁.
- Edge x = B₃–Q → class {B₀,B₃}–{B₁,Q}
- Edge 5 = B₀–B₁ → class {B₀,B₃}–{B₁,Q}

These are the same octahedron edge, so x meets edge 5 (just as 3 meets 2, and 4 meets 1).

\boxed{5}
